sections	
0	
label	"Section 1"
heading	"Executive summary"
paragraphs	
0	`The client proposed naming the band after its two lead singers. We tested this name with twelve members of the public. Eleven of them believed they were being asked about an Irish folk cover band. The twelfth asked whether this was a law firm and, when told it was not, said "that's a shame."`
1	'We do not recommend proceeding with "McCarty &amp; McCullough." We recommend the alternative described in Section 5, which tested as "alliterative, but in a way that made people hungry."'
1	
label	"Section 2"
heading	"Method"
paragraphs	
0	"Twelve participants were recruited from the parking lot of a grocery store in Lincoln, Nebraska, with the offer of a free doughnut. Each was shown the name on a plain white card and asked to describe, in their own words, what the band probably sounded like. Participants were then shown a photograph of the band (Appendix B) and asked whether it changed their answer. It did not."
1	'One participant left before the study concluded, citing "the fiddle thing." His partial responses are included in Appendix A.'
